    "Most schools of philosophy are a person and their pupils: a generation or two, a set of doctrines, a slow fade as the next thing arrives. Platonism is not that. It is the longest continuous argument in Western thought, running from a grove outside Athens in the fourth century BCE to seminar rooms in the philosophy of mathematics today, and the strange thing about it is that the people carrying it forward kept disagreeing, violently, about what it actually said. Plato's own school spent two centuries arguing that Plato was wrong. Christian theologians built their God out of a pagan's metaphysics. A fifteenth-century scholar gave Europe back a Plato it had not read in a thousand years. And in the twentieth century, mathematicians who had never thought of themselves as Plato's heirs found they were defending his oldest claim in a new dialect.",
