      "The Forms are an answer to a genuine deadlock, not a fantasy invented for its own sake. Two earlier thinkers had argued the Greek world into a corner. Heraclitus held that everything is in flux (no one can step into the same river twice, because the river is never the same river) and if everything is always changing, then there is nothing stable to know, and knowledge is impossible. Parmenides argued the reverse: change is literally inconceivable, because for something to change it would have to come from what is not, which is nothing, and nothing cannot give rise to anything; so only unchanging Being is real, and the senses, which show us a world of change, are simply lying to us. Both arguments were strong. Together they left knowledge looking either impossible or unavailable, and the Sophists drew the obvious lesson: if nothing can really be known, then wisdom is just persuasion, and the clever talker wins.",
